---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';

const sections = [
  {
    title: 'Practice',
    copy: 'The current inward capability laboratory across scholarliness, software engineering, engineering leadership and Nemosyne.',
    href: '/practice/',
    label: 'Practice',
  },
  {
    title: 'Explore',
    copy: 'The wider knowledge space, including earlier research directions, notes, methods and evidence that remain part of the lineage.',
    href: '/explore/',
    label: 'Explore',
  },
  {
    title: 'Principles',
    copy: 'Operating commitments for defensibility, openness, contribution, publication and correction.',
    href: '/principles/',
    label: 'Principles',
  },
  {
    title: 'Intellectual lineage',
    copy: 'The living prior-art and novelty register, including ideas that were narrowed or renamed after review.',
    href: '/lineage/',
    label: 'Lineage',
  },
  {
    title: 'Research provenance',
    copy: 'Standards-first contribution and process records using W3C PROV, CRediT and RO-Crate where possible.',
    href: '/provenance/',
    label: 'Provenance',
  },
  {
    title: 'Memory Palace Protocol',
    copy: 'The session protocol for preserving questions, branches, decisions, roads not taken, evidence and contribution history.',
    href: '/memory-palace-protocol/',
    label: 'Memory Palace',
  },
  {
    title: 'astro-agalmic',
    copy: 'A freely reusable Astro integration extracted from the site: semantic registry, validation, static search, history and generated context.',
    href: '/tools/astro-agalmic/',
    label: 'Take the integration',
  },
];
---
<Layout title="About" description="About Agalmic Research: an open capability practice that begins inwardly, inherits existing knowledge and contributes useful outputs without making novelty the primary goal.">
  <header class="page-head">
    <div class="shell">
      <p class="eyebrow">About the programme</p>
      <h1>A laboratory for turning available abundance into durable capability.</h1>
      <p class="lede">Agalmic Research began as an attempt to understand abundance, shifting scarcity and machine-assisted discovery. It is now deliberately more modest: apply those ideas first to real constraints in the practitioner's own work, learn from what already exists, and let outward contributions emerge where they genuinely add capability.</p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell split">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Current orientation</p>
        <h2>Practice before proclamation.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p class="large">The programme does not need to establish a new academic field in order to be useful.</p>
        <p>Its present role is to identify real constraints, search the inherited stock of knowledge and tools, use abundant resources such as machine cognition against those constraints, and test whether capability actually increased.</p>
        <p>Research methods remain important because they keep the practice answerable to lineage, evidence, criticism, provenance and correction. They serve capability rather than intellectual territoriality.</p>
        <div class="actions">
          <a class="button" href="/practice/">See the current practice</a>
          <a class="button secondary" href="/definition/">Read definition v0.3</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell split">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Contribution</p>
        <h2>We do not need to own an idea to advance it.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>Prior art, mature scholarship, standards and open-source software reduce the amount of discovery that still needs to be paid for. Finding them is therefore a gain in available capability.</p>
        <p>When this project improves something inherited, the preferred response is to preserve lineage and, where practical, contribute the improvement back. Novelty may emerge, but it is not a prerequisite for useful work.</p>
        <div class="callout"><p><strong>Seek contribution before novelty. Seek novelty only where contribution requires discovery.</strong></p></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell">
      <div class="section-head">
        <span class="section-number">Under the hood</span>
        <div class="section-copy">
          <h2>Practice, research machinery and history.</h2>
          <p>The site preserves earlier framings rather than silently erasing them. They remain useful evidence of how the programme changed.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="about-grid">
        {sections.map((item) => (
          <article class="about-card">
            <h3>{item.title}</h3>
            <p>{item.copy}</p>
            <a href={item.href}>{item.label} →</a>
          </article>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section">
    <div class="shell split">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Open source</p>
        <h2>The website is part of the working record.</h2>
      </div>
      <div class="prose">
        <p>The site source, provenance records, literature reviews, portfolio data and machine-readable indexes live in the public repository. Public source does not mean every future artefact must be public: confidential, personally sensitive, security-sensitive or consciously protected material can remain outside the public projection.</p>
        <p>Reusable infrastructure should travel farther than the site that produced it. The <strong>astro-agalmic</strong> integration is published under 0BSD so others can take useful machinery without inheriting this project's vocabulary.</p>
        <div class="actions">
          <a class="button secondary" href="https://github.com/TsatsuAmable/agalmicresearch">GitHub source ↗</a>
          <a class="button secondary" href="/tools/astro-agalmic/">astro-agalmic</a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
